<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What Budget Home Fixes is, how our cost guides are researched, and how to report an error."
>
  <h1>About Budget Home Fixes</h1>
  <p>
    Budget Home Fixes publishes cost breakdowns and side-by-side comparisons for home upgrades, aimed at renters
    and anyone working with a tight budget. Each guide tries to answer one question: what will this actually cost,
    and is there a cheaper or safer alternative?
  </p>

  <h2>How our guides are made</h2>
  <p>
    We compile price ranges and specifications from published sources: cost guides, manufacturer and retailer pages,
    and, for rules such as security deposits, official or well-established legal information sites. Every guide ends
    with a list of the pages we used and what we took from each. Where sources disagree, we show the spread instead
    of picking one number, and we label any figure that is our own calculation or estimate.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not test products ourselves, and we do not accept payment to rank or recommend anything. Articles are
    researched and written with the help of AI tools, working from the sources they cite.
  </p>

  <h2>Use the numbers as a starting point</h2>
  <p>
    Prices change with sales, region and the condition of your home, and rental rules vary by state and by lease.
    Treat our figures as planning ranges rather than quotes, and get written quotes from professionals before you
    hire anyone. Nothing here is legal, financial or contracting advice.
  </p>

  <h2>Spot an error?</h2>
  <p>
    If a price, figure or source looks wrong or out of date, email
    <a href="mailto:hello@thebudgethomefixes.com">hello@thebudgethomefixes.com</a> and we will check it and correct
    the guide.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
